Article 21

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women from acts of violence, the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. Ministries;

b. Provincial Regional Government;

c. Regency/City Regional Government;

d. Universities; and

e. Non-governmental institutions.

(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. exchange of data and information;

b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;

c. repatriation and social reintegration; and

d. provision of evidence and witnesses.

Article 7

(1) In the protection of women, violence prevention is carried out. (2) Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by: a. socializing laws and regulations;

b. providing education on the dangers of violence against women; c. conducting seminars/workshops or similar activities;

d. establishing cooperation with government agencies or non-government institutions in violence prevention efforts; and

e. establishing a prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence.

(3) Violence prevention as referred to in paragraph (2) may coordinate with other institutions.

3.5.text:
Article 9

Types of services for victims of violence include:

a. complaint and consultation services;
b. assistance services;
c. legal aid services;
d. health services;
e. social rehabilitation services;
f. repatriation services; and
g. social reintegration services.

Article 13

(1) Assistance Services as referred to in Article 9 letter b, are provided in each type of health service, rehabilitation, legal aid, social reintegration, and repatriation.

(2) Assistance is provided by experts who have expertise in conducting counseling, therapy and/or advocacy for the empowerment and recovery of women victims of violence.

(3) Experts who provide assistance as referred to in paragraph (2) cooperate with UPTD PPA.

(4) Experts as referred to in paragraph (2) must be provided by the Regional Apparatus responsible for women.

(5) Experts as referred to in paragraph (2) consist of: a. health workers;

b. advocates;

c. social workers;

d. spiritual counselors; and

e. psychologists or psychiatrists.

Article 4

Women victims of violence have the right to:

a. have their dignity and honor respected as human beings;
b. obtain information on the protection of women;
c. obtain social rehabilitation and reintegration;
d. obtain physical, psychological and sexual health recovery according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
e. obtain empowerment and legal assistance; and f. spiritual guidance services.

